C = {
    "belgium":{"name":"Belgium","code":"belgium","iso":"BE","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"Flanders offers a reduced 3% registration duty (down from 6%) for primary residences, saving buyers thousands of euros. Brussels provides an abatement scheme exempting the first €200,000 from registration duties on purchases up to €600,000. Wallonia offers reduced registration for modest homes. Energy renovation grants are available through each region — Flanders' Mijn VerbouwPremie, Brussels' RENOLUTION, and Wallonia's audit-linked premiums cover insulation, heating, and renewable energy installations","refinance":"Belgian early repayment penalties (wederbeleggingsvergoeding) are capped at 3 months' interest on the repaid amount. However, refinancing involves new notary fees and registration tax for the replacement mortgage. A rate difference of at least 1% is typically needed to justify the costs. Compare the total saving over the remaining term against all switching costs before committing","investment":"Belgium offers stable rental yields of 3-5% in major cities. Brussels, Antwerp, and Ghent have strong tenant demand. Registration duties are higher for non-primary residences. Rental income is taxed through the cadastral system (forfaitaire belasting) for Belgian tenants or on actual income for commercial/furnished rentals. The revenu cadastral indexé forms the tax base","inspection":"Belgian homes are sold 'as seen' unless specific guarantees are in the contract. Key checks: structural integrity (foundation, walls, roof), damp and moisture issues (common in older Belgian townhouses), EPC (Energy Performance Certificate) score, electrical installation compliance certificate (AREI/RGIE), fuel tank certificate if applicable, and asbestos assessment for pre-2001 buildings","mistakes":"Not understanding regional registration duty differences (Flanders 3% vs Wallonia/Brussels 12.5%), skipping the building inspection because it is not mandatory, not budgeting for the schuldsaldoverzekering (outstanding balance insurance), rushing the compromis (preliminary contract) without lawyer review, and not comparing mortgage rates across enough banks","rentVsBuy":"With registration duties of 3-12.5% depending on region, buying has high entry costs. Belgian rental protections are strong with regulated lease terms (typically 3/6/9 year contracts). At current rates and prices, buying becomes financially advantageous after approximately 5-8 years in Flanders (lower duties) and 8-12 years in Wallonia/Brussels. The key factors are registration duty, mortgage rate, expected appreciation (historically 2-4% annually), and your expected duration of stay","propertyTax":"Onroerende voorheffing/précompte immobilier (annual property tax) is calculated on the indexed cadastral income at rates set by the region, province, and municipality — typically 20-55% of the indexed KI. Flanders' registration duty is 3% for primary residences (12% otherwise). Wallonia and Brussels charge 12.5%. Mortgage registration tax is approximately 1% of the loan amount. Capital gains on your primary residence are tax-free after 5 years of ownership"},
    "brazil":{"name":"Brazil","code":"brazil","iso":"BR","sym":"R$","cb":"Central Bank of Brazil","govGrants":"Minha Casa Minha Vida is the cornerstone programme — subsidised rates of 4-5% for families earning up to R$8,000/month (versus 9-12% market rates). FGTS (Workers' Severance Fund) can be used for deposits, monthly payments, or balance reduction every 2 years. Federal subsidies provide direct grants for lower-income buyers reducing the purchase price. FGTS Pro-Cotista offers below-market rates for contributors who do not qualify for Minha Casa Minha Vida","refinance":"Portabilidade de crédito (mortgage portability) allows free transfer to a competing bank. Your current bank has 5 business days to match the offer. The process became easier since 2019 and costs nothing. Given Brazil's high rates (8-12%), even a 1% reduction saves substantially. Use FGTS balance every 2 years to reduce principal — this is effectively a refinancing tool that costs nothing","investment":"Brazilian rental yields average 4-6% in major cities. São Paulo and Rio premium areas attract strong demand. IPTU (property tax) and condominium fees reduce net returns. Rental income is taxed progressively at 7.5-27.5% through the carnê-leão system. Capital gains tax of 15-22.5% applies on sale. Consider FII (Fundos de Investimento Imobiliário) as a more liquid alternative to direct property investment","inspection":"For under-construction: verify RERA registration, check the developer's CERSAI records, confirm habite-se (occupancy certificate) timeline. For resale: obtain matrícula atualizada (updated property certificate) from the Cartório, verify IPTU payments, check for débitos condominiais (outstanding condominium charges), inspect for structural issues especially in older buildings, verify water and sewage connections, and check for regularisation of any modifications","mistakes":"Not checking Caixa/FGTS eligibility first (losing access to subsidised rates), choosing PRICE over SAC amortisation (paying significantly more total interest), not verifying RERA registration for under-construction properties, not using FGTS to reduce the balance every 2 years, and underestimating ITBI and cartório fees","rentVsBuy":"Brazilian mortgage rates of 8-12% (4-5% with FGTS subsidy) are high, making the buy-vs-rent calculation highly dependent on whether you qualify for subsidised financing. With FGTS rates, buying is attractive after 3-5 years. At commercial rates, the breakeven extends to 8-12+ years. Rental yields of 4-6% mean monthly rent is often lower than mortgage payments at market rates. Key factors: FGTS eligibility, expected stay duration, local appreciation trends, and access to Minha Casa Minha Vida","propertyTax":"ITBI (transfer tax) of 2-3% at purchase. IPTU (annual property tax) varies by municipality — São Paulo charges approximately 1% of the venal value. Rental income is taxed at 7.5-27.5% (progressive). Capital gains tax of 15-22.5% on profit from sale (with exemptions for primary residence if buying another within 180 days). ITR (rural land tax) applies to non-urban property. FGTS-funded purchases may have reduced ITBI in some municipalities"},
    "canada":{"name":"Canada","code":"canada","iso":"CA","sym":"$","cb":"Bank of Canada","govGrants":"The First Home Savings Account (FHSA) allows tax-deductible savings of $8,000/year ($40,000 lifetime). The Home Buyers' Plan (HBP) lets you withdraw $60,000 from RRSP tax-free. Ontario first-time buyers get land transfer tax rebate up to $4,000. BC offers a partial exemption up to $500,000. The First-Time Home Buyer Tax Credit provides $1,500 federal tax relief. GST/HST New Housing Rebate for new builds","refinance":"You can refinance up to 80% of appraised value. Fixed-rate early termination penalty is the greater of 3 months' interest or the Interest Rate Differential (IRD) — this can be thousands of dollars. Variable-rate penalty is typically just 3 months' interest. At renewal (every 1-5 years), switching lenders is free. Blend-and-extend with your current lender avoids penalties. Always shop at renewal — your bank's first offer is negotiable","investment":"Canadian rental yields average 3-5% in major markets. Rental income is taxed at your marginal rate. Expenses (mortgage interest, property tax, insurance, maintenance, management fees) are deductible against rental income. Capital gains: 50% of the gain is taxable at your marginal rate. The principal residence exemption eliminates capital gains on your primary home. 1% Underused Housing Tax applies to vacant or underused property owned by non-residents","inspection":"Professional home inspection covers structure, roof, foundation, plumbing, electrical, HVAC, windows/doors, insulation, and exterior grading. Most offers include an inspection condition (though competitive markets see waivers — risky). Additional checks: well water quality and septic system (rural), radon testing, vermiculite insulation (asbestos risk), UFFI (urea formaldehyde foam insulation), underground oil tanks, and flood zone mapping. Condos: review status certificate including reserve fund, bylaws, and financial statements","mistakes":"Waiving the home inspection in bidding wars, not understanding the stress test (reduces borrowing by 15-20%), forgetting CMHC insurance costs (2.8-4% added to loan for <20% down), not shopping rates at renewal, and underestimating Toronto's double land transfer tax","rentVsBuy":"With 5-year fixed rates around 4-5% and property taxes of 0.5-2%, the buy decision depends heavily on location. In Toronto and Vancouver where prices are very high, renting and investing the difference can be competitive with buying for stays under 5-7 years. In mid-tier cities (Calgary, Ottawa, Halifax), buying is favourable after 3-5 years. The mortgage stress test reduces maximum borrowing, making the calculation location-specific. Use our calculator to model your scenario","propertyTax":"Land transfer tax varies by province: Ontario 0.5-2.5% (Toronto adds municipal LTT), BC 1-5%, Alberta zero. Annual property tax of 0.5-2% of assessed value varies by municipality. Rental income taxed at marginal rate (deductible expenses offset this). Capital gains: 50% inclusion rate at marginal tax. Principal residence exemption applies to your primary home. Non-resident speculation tax of 25% in Ontario and BC on foreign buyers"},
    "denmark":{"name":"Denmark","code":"denmark","iso":"DK","sym":"kr","cb":"Danmarks Nationalbank","govGrants":"Denmark has limited direct buyer subsidies. Mortgage interest tax deductibility at approximately 33% (lower above kr 50,000/person). Some municipalities offer discounted building plots to attract new residents. Energy renovation grants are available through the Energy Agency for improving insulation, heating, and windows. The covered bond system itself provides implicit support through some of Europe's lowest mortgage rates","refinance":"Denmark's covered bond system makes refinancing unique. Fixed-rate borrowers repay by buying back their bonds at market price — if rates have risen, bonds trade below par and you can profit by retiring your mortgage at a discount. For adjustable-rate loans, refinancing happens automatically at each renewal auction. The bidrag (administration fee) varies between mortgage banks and can be renegotiated. No penalty for fixed-rate prepayment (you buy back bonds at market price)","investment":"Danish rental yields average 3-5% in Copenhagen, higher in smaller cities. Rental income is taxed as personal income. Capital gains on investment property are taxed as personal income. The ejendomsværdiskat (property value tax) applies to all owned properties. Strict rent control (huslejenævn) in older Copenhagen apartments limits rental increases. Newer apartments have more flexible pricing","inspection":"The tilstandsrapport (condition report) is provided by the seller and prepared by a certified inspector. Review it carefully for structural issues, roof condition, moisture/damp, drainage, electrical, and heating systems. Consider purchasing ejerskifteforsikring (change-of-ownership insurance) based on the report — it covers hidden defects. For older properties, check for asbestos, PCB, and lead paint. Verify energimærke (energy label) — Denmark's cold climate makes heating efficiency critical","mistakes":"Not understanding the two-layer financing (realkredit + bank loan at different rates), underestimating ongoing bidrag (0.5-1.5%/year), rushing into the budrunde without proper financing, not getting ejerskifteforsikring when the tilstandsrapport flags issues, and not comparing between mortgage banks (Nykredit vs Realkredit Danmark rates differ)","rentVsBuy":"Denmark's low mortgage rates (through covered bonds) make buying very competitive with renting. In Copenhagen, buying typically becomes advantageous after 3-5 years considering the 5% minimum deposit, registration duties, and mortgage interest deductibility. Outside Copenhagen, the calculus favours buying even sooner due to lower prices. The key advantage of Danish ownership is the mortgage interest tax deduction and the ability to lock in a 30-year fixed rate at competitive levels","propertyTax":"Tinglysningsafgift (registration duty) of 0.6% + kr 1,850 at purchase. Ejendomsværdiskat (property value tax) of 0.51% on values up to kr 3,040,000, 1.4% above that (based on 2001/2002 valuations, transitioning to new system). Grundskyld (land tax) varies by municipality (0.16-3.4‰). Mortgage interest is tax-deductible at approximately 33%. Capital gains on your primary residence are tax-free if you have lived there for the required period"},
    "france":{"name":"France","code":"france","iso":"FR","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"The PTZ (Prêt à Taux Zéro) provides zero-interest financing of up to 40% of the purchase price for first-time buyers of new-build or qualifying renovated properties. Income limits apply by zone and household size. The PAS (Prêt d'Accession Sociale) offers subsidised rates for lower-income buyers. Action Logement provides employer-assisted loans at reduced rates for employees of companies with 10+ staff. MaPrimeRénov' provides renovation grants for energy efficiency improvements","refinance":"The IRA (Indemnités de Remboursement Anticipé) is capped at the lesser of 3% of outstanding capital or 6 months' interest — making refinancing calculable. Given France's competitive fixed rates, refinancing makes sense when rates have dropped significantly (typically 0.7%+ below your current rate). The Lemoine law allows switching your assurance emprunteur (borrower's insurance) at any time — often saving 0.1-0.3% without refinancing the loan itself","investment":"French rental yields average 3-5% in major cities, with Paris at the lower end and cities like Lyon, Bordeaux, and Nantes offering better returns. The Pinel scheme previously offered tax reductions for buy-to-let in qualifying areas (check current status). Rental income is taxed under micro-foncier (30% abatement for gross income under €15,000) or régime réel (actual expenses deductible). LMNP (Location Meublée Non Professionnelle) for furnished rentals offers favourable tax treatment with depreciation deductions","inspection":"Mandatory seller diagnostics (DDT — Dossier de Diagnostics Techniques) include: DPE (energy performance), amiante (asbestos, pre-1997 buildings), plomb (lead, pre-1949), termites (in designated zones), ERP (natural/technological risks), electricity (over 15 years), gas (over 15 years), and assainissement (sanitation). These are the seller's obligation. Buyers should additionally inspect for structural issues, humidity, roof condition, and compliance with planning regulations — especially important for rural properties","mistakes":"Not checking PTZ eligibility before committing to a property type or location, underestimating frais de notaire (7-8% for existing, 2-3% for new), exceeding the 35% taux d'endettement limit, accepting the bank's assurance emprunteur without comparing external providers, and not using the 10-day cooling-off period after signing the compromis to verify everything","rentVsBuy":"French frais de notaire of 7-8% for existing properties create high entry costs. However, long-term fixed rates at competitive levels make monthly mortgage payments predictable and often comparable to rent. In Paris, high prices mean buying typically becomes advantageous only after 8-12+ years. In medium-sized cities, the breakeven is 4-7 years. New builds have lower frais de notaire (2-3%) and may qualify for PTZ, accelerating the breakeven. The mortgage interest is not tax-deductible for primary residences in France","propertyTax":"Frais de notaire (acquisition taxes) of 7-8% for existing, 2-3% for new. Taxe foncière (annual property tax, paid by owner) varies by commune — €500-5,000+/year. Taxe d'habitation on secondary residences. Plus-value immobilière (capital gains tax) of 19% + 17.2% social charges on investment property, with tapering relief after 6 years (full exemption at 22 years for tax, 30 years for social charges). Primary residence gains are fully exempt"},
    "germany":{"name":"Germany","code":"germany","iso":"DE","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"KfW Bank offers subsidised loans for energy-efficient construction and renovation through programmes like KfW 261 — below-market rates plus repayment bonuses for achieving high energy standards. Some Bundesländer have their own programmes (BayernLabo, NRW.BANK, L-Bank). Wohn-Riester allows using state-subsidised retirement savings toward mortgage payments. BAFA grants support renewable energy installations. The Baukindergeld (child benefit for building) has ended but may be succeeded by new programmes","refinance":"The Vorfälligkeitsentschädigung (prepayment penalty) during the Zinsbindung can be substantial — banks calculate their lost interest for the remaining fixed period. However, under §489 BGB, you can exit any fixed-rate loan after 10 years with 6 months' notice and zero penalty. Plan your Anschlussfinanzierung 12-36 months early using a Forward-Darlehen to lock in rates. At Zinsbindung expiry, switching lenders is free — always compare","investment":"German rental yields average 3-5% with strong tenant demand in major cities. Mietpreisbremse (rent control) limits rent increases in designated areas to 10% above the local reference rent. Rental income is taxed at your personal rate minus deductible expenses (mortgage interest, depreciation at 2-3%/year, management, maintenance). Grunderwerbsteuer (3.5-6.5%) applies on purchase. Spekulationssteuer (speculation tax) on capital gains if sold within 10 years","inspection":"Professional Baugutachten (building surveys) are not standard but highly recommended. Key checks: structural integrity (Statik), moisture and mould (common in older buildings), roof condition, heating system efficiency, electrical wiring compliance, energy certificate (Energieausweis), asbestos and lead in pre-1993 buildings. For apartments: review Hausgeldabrechnung (management accounts), Instandhaltungsrücklage (maintenance reserve), and Teilungserklärung (division declaration). Verify Grundbuch (land registry) for any Belastungen (encumbrances)","mistakes":"Underestimating Kaufnebenkosten (10-15% from savings), not checking SCHUFA early, choosing too short a Zinsbindung, not using a Vermittler (broker) to compare 400+ lenders, not factoring in Grundsteuer reform impacts, and not understanding the §489 BGB 10-year exit right","rentVsBuy":"Germany's historically low homeownership rate (around 50%) reflects a strong rental culture with excellent tenant protections. Buying becomes financially attractive after 8-15 years considering the high Kaufnebenkosten (10-15%). In Munich and Frankfurt with high prices, the breakeven extends further. In eastern German cities with lower prices, buying can be advantageous sooner. The mortgage interest is not tax-deductible for owner-occupiers in Germany, unlike investment properties","propertyTax":"Grunderwerbsteuer (transfer tax) of 3.5-6.5% depending on Bundesland. Maklergebühr (agent commission) of 3-3.5% (buyer's share since 2020 reform). Notar und Grundbuch (notary and registry) of ~1.5-2%. Annual Grundsteuer (property tax) reformed in 2025 — varies by municipality. Rental income taxed at personal rate with deductions for depreciation (2-3%/year), interest, and expenses. Capital gains exempt after 10 years of ownership (Spekulationsfrist)"},
    "hong-kong":{"name":"Hong Kong","code":"hong-kong","iso":"HK","sym":"$","cb":"Hong Kong Monetary Authority","govGrants":"Home Ownership Scheme (HOS) sells flats at 30-40% below market value to eligible permanent residents. Green Form Subsidised Home Ownership (GSH) targets public housing tenants at 40-50% discounts. White Form Secondary Market Scheme (WFSM) allows buying HOS resale without premium. HKMC Mortgage Insurance Programme (MIP) enables up to 90% LTV. Public housing tenants can buy their unit through the Tenants Purchase Scheme at significant discounts","refinance":"Refinancing (轉按) between banks is common and incentivised with cash rebates of 1-2% of the loan. After the 2-3 year clawback period, switching is penalty-free and the new bank typically offers another cash rebate. Many Hong Kong borrowers refinance every 2-3 years systematically. Compare H-plan offers across HSBC, Hang Seng, BOC, and Standard Chartered. Mortgage brokers like mReferral aggregate the best current deals","investment":"Hong Kong rental yields are low (2-3%) relative to property prices but demand is strong due to limited supply. Stamp duty is punitive for non-first/second properties (15% for second+ property for residents). Property tax of 15% on net rental income applies. Capital gains are not separately taxed in Hong Kong. The market is cyclical and heavily influenced by government cooling measures and interest rate movements","inspection":"For older buildings: check for unauthorised building works (UBW) through the Buildings Department, review building orders, inspect common areas and building maintenance condition. For all properties: moisture and water seepage (very common in Hong Kong's humid climate), window condition (mandatory Window Inspection Scheme for buildings over 10 years), slope safety adjacent to the building, and lift maintenance records. Review the Deed of Mutual Covenant (DMC) for strata properties","mistakes":"Not applying to multiple banks for competing offers, underestimating stamp duty (especially the 15% rate for non-first-time buyers), not checking the foreign ownership quota (49%) in a building before buying a condo unit, rushing into off-plan without understanding completion risk, and not factoring in management fees (HK$1,500-5,000/month)","rentVsBuy":"Hong Kong has one of the highest price-to-income ratios globally (20x+), making the buy-vs-rent decision complex. Government subsidised housing (HOS/GSH) fundamentally changes the equation for eligible residents. For private housing, buying becomes advantageous after 7-12+ years considering stamp duty and high prices. Monthly mortgage payments (even with MIP) often exceed comparable rent. The key factor is whether you qualify for subsidised housing, which is the most financially advantageous path to ownership","propertyTax":"Ad Valorem Stamp Duty: Scale 2 (1.5-4.25%) for first-time permanent residents, 15% flat for non-first-time. Government rates of 5% of rateable value quarterly. Government rent for leasehold property of 3% of rateable value. Property tax of 15% of net rental income for let properties. No separate capital gains tax. Annual management fees vary by building"},
    "india":{"name":"India","code":"india","iso":"IN","sym":"₹","cb":"Reserve Bank of India","govGrants":"PMAY (Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana) provides credit-linked interest subsidy up to ₹2.67 lakh for EWS/LIG/MIG. Section 80C allows ₹1.5 lakh deduction on principal repayment. Section 24(b) provides ₹2 lakh deduction on interest for self-occupied property. First-time buyers get additional benefits under various state schemes. State-specific programmes: MHADA (Maharashtra), DDA (Delhi), BDA (Bangalore) offer affordable housing through lottery","refinance":"Balance transfer is free for floating-rate loans (zero prepayment penalty — RBI mandated). If another lender offers a lower rate, transfer at no cost. Even a 0.25% reduction saves lakhs over 15-20 years. Compare rates annually on BankBazaar, PaisaBazaar, or MyLoanCare. Fixed-rate loans may carry up to 2% penalty. Top-up loans allow additional borrowing on existing property without full refinancing","investment":"Indian rental yields average 2-4% in major cities. Rental income above ₹2.5 lakh (old regime) is taxed at slab rate with 30% standard deduction for repairs. Capital gains: short-term (under 2 years) at slab rate, long-term at 20% with indexation. Section 54 allows exemption by reinvesting in another property. TDS of 1% on property transactions above ₹50 lakh. NRIs face 20.8% TDS on long-term capital gains","inspection":"For under-construction: verify RERA registration on the state RERA portal, check developer track record, confirm occupancy certificate timeline, review CERSAI charge records. For resale: obtain encumbrance certificate for 30+ years, verify khata/patta records, check property tax clearance, confirm no pending litigation through revenue records. Physical inspection should cover structural integrity, water supply, electrical wiring, seepage/dampness, and legal compliance of all constructions. Engage an independent lawyer — do not rely on the builder's legal team","mistakes":"Not checking RERA registration for under-construction projects, not comparing rates across 3-4+ lenders, forgetting stamp duty (3-8%) and registration (1%), accepting a high rate without negotiating or checking CIBIL score first, not using balance transfer when better rates are available, and trusting builder promises without verifying documents","rentVsBuy":"Indian home loan rates of 8-9% (floating) are high relative to rental yields of 2-4%, making the pure financial case for buying weaker than in low-rate markets. However, the tax benefits (Section 80C + 24b saving ₹60,000-80,000/year in tax) and long-term appreciation (5-10% annually in good locations) shift the balance. Buying becomes financially superior after 5-8 years in most major cities when including tax savings and appreciation. EMI-to-rent ratios above 2x suggest renting may be preferable in the short term","propertyTax":"Stamp duty of 3-8% by state. Registration of 1% in most states. GST of 5% on under-construction only. Annual property tax varies by municipal corporation. Rental income taxed at slab rate with 30% standard deduction. Capital gains: LTCG at 20% with indexation (after 2 years), STCG at slab rate. TDS of 1% on transactions above ₹50 lakh. Wealth tax abolished but annual property tax is ongoing"},
    "ireland":{"name":"Ireland","code":"ireland","iso":"IE","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"Help to Buy (HTB) provides up to €30,000 tax refund for first-time buyers of new homes. First Home Scheme offers up to 30% shared equity on new builds. Local Authority Home Loan provides below-market fixed rates for lower-income buyers. Mortgage interest relief was discontinued but may be reintroduced. SEAI grants cover energy upgrades for existing homes","refinance":"Switching lenders has become more common with cashback offers of up to 2% for switchers. The process takes 8-12 weeks involving new application, valuation, and legal work (€1,500-2,500). Factor in legal costs against savings. If you have a legacy ECB tracker mortgage, never switch — these cannot be replaced and are worth thousands per year. Green mortgage rates offer 0.1-0.2% discounts for BER A/B properties","investment":"Irish rental yields average 4-6% with exceptionally strong demand due to housing shortage. Rental income is taxed at marginal rate (up to 52% including USC/PRSI). Deductible expenses: mortgage interest (100%), management fees, insurance, repairs. Capital gains at 33%. LPT (Local Property Tax) applies. Rent Pressure Zones cap rent increases at 2% annually. Non-resident landlords face 20% withholding tax on gross rent","inspection":"Three survey types: Level 1 (condition report — basic), Level 2 (HomeBuyer survey — mid-level, recommended for most purchases), Level 3 (building survey — full structural, for older or unusual properties). Key Irish-specific checks: pyrite contamination (particularly in certain Dublin/Leinster areas), moisture and dampness, BER rating (legally required), septic tank compliance (rural), radon levels (check radon map), and planning permission compliance for extensions/conversions","mistakes":"Not understanding the LTI cap (4x FTB, 3.5x SSB) as the binding constraint, not applying for HTB before committing to a new build, not checking Avant Money and Finance Ireland alongside main banks, underestimating how fast Dublin properties sell, and not getting a proper building survey (particularly for pyrite risk areas)","rentVsBuy":"Ireland's housing shortage means rents are very high relative to historical norms, making buying more attractive than in balanced markets. With stamp duty of just 1%, entry costs are low by European standards. The LTI cap (4x income) is the main constraint, not the deposit. Buying becomes financially advantageous after just 3-5 years in most areas considering low stamp duty, strong appreciation (3-6% annually), and high rental costs. The key constraint is access to supply — available properties are limited","propertyTax":"Stamp duty of 1% up to €1M, 2% above (one of the lowest in Europe). LPT (Local Property Tax) based on value bands — typically €200-1,000+ annually. Rental income at marginal rate (up to 52%). Capital gains at 33% (with €1,270 annual exemption). PRSI of 4% on rental income. TRS (mortgage interest relief) was discontinued. Buildings and contents insurance are ongoing costs"},
    "italy":{"name":"Italy","code":"italy","iso":"IT","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"Fondo di Garanzia Prima Casa provides 80% state guarantee enabling up to 100% LTV for under-36 buyers with ISEE below €40,000 — plus exemption from registration, mortgage, and cadastral taxes. Superbonus (originally 110%, now reduced) provided tax deductions for energy efficiency and seismic upgrades. Ecobonus offers 50-65% deductions for energy improvements. Bonus ristrutturazione provides 50% deduction for renovations. Check current availability as percentages change annually","refinance":"Italy's surroga (portabilità del mutuo) is uniquely powerful — completely free to the borrower, with the new bank absorbing all costs including notary. You can change rate type, term, and lender at zero cost, unlimited times. This is separate from sostituzione (full replacement) which allows increasing the loan amount but has costs. Use MutuiOnline.it to find the best surroga offer. After the 2022-2023 rate spike, many borrowers used surroga to switch from variable to fixed","investment":"Italian rental yields average 4-7% depending on location — southern Italy and tourist areas offer higher yields. Short-term rentals (Airbnb) face increasing regulation with mandatory CIN codes. Cedolare secca (flat tax) of 21% on residential rental income (26% for short-term rentals from 2024) simplifies taxation. IMU (property tax) applies to non-primary residences at 0.76-1.06% of cadastral value. Capital gains tax at 26% if sold within 5 years (primary residence exempt)","inspection":"Italian inspections should cover: conformità urbanistica e catastale (verification that the property matches planning permissions and cadastral records — critical in Italy where irregular modifications are common), structural integrity, electrical and plumbing compliance, roof and facade condition, common area maintenance for apartments, APE (energy certificate — mandatory for sale), and certificato di agibilità (habitability certificate). Engage a geometra or architetto for technical due diligence","mistakes":"Not checking Fondo di Garanzia eligibility if under 36, not understanding cadastral vs market value for tax calculations, not verifying conformità urbanistica (irregular modifications create huge legal problems), underestimating notary costs, and not using the free surroga to switch lenders","rentVsBuy":"Italy offers relatively affordable property compared to northern Europe, with favourable tax treatment for primary residences. Buying costs from a private seller (2% of cadastral value + notary) are reasonable. At current fixed rates (3-4%), buying becomes advantageous after 4-7 years. The Fondo di Garanzia for under-36 buyers makes buying exceptionally attractive with zero deposit and tax exemptions. Key consideration: Italian property values in many areas have not recovered to 2008 peaks, so appreciation expectations should be moderate","propertyTax":"Imposta di registro: 2% of cadastral value for primary residence (from private), 9% for non-primary. IVA: 4% of full price for primary (from developer), 10% non-primary. Under-36 FTBs: exempt from registration taxes. IMU: exempt for primary residence, 0.76-1.06% of cadastral value for others. TARI (waste tax) is annual. Cedolare secca: 21% flat tax on rental income. Capital gains: 26% if sold within 5 years (primary exempt)"},
    "japan":{"name":"Japan","code":"japan","iso":"JP","sym":"¥","cb":"Bank of Japan","govGrants":"Housing loan tax deduction (住宅ローン控除) of 0.7% of remaining balance annually for 13 years (new homes) or 10 years (existing). Flat 35S provides 0.25% rate discount for first 5-10 years on quality-certified homes. ZEH subsidies for net-zero energy houses. Sumai-Kyufu-Kin grants based on income level. Local municipality subsidies vary — some offer cash grants for relocating to rural areas. Renovation grants for earthquake resistance and energy efficiency upgrades","refinance":"No prepayment penalty on variable-rate mortgages at most banks. Fixed-rate loans may carry a small penalty. Online banks (SBI Sumishin) often offer the lowest refinancing rates. The main costs are new registration tax, guarantee fees (if applicable), and judicial scrivener fees. Given Japan's ultra-low rates, refinancing saves most when switching from an older fixed rate to current lower rates","investment":"Japanese rental yields average 4-6% in Tokyo, higher in regional cities. Rental income is taxed at progressive rates (5-45%). Depreciation deductions (wooden: 22 years, RC: 47 years) significantly reduce taxable rental income. Capital gains: short-term (under 5 years) at approximately 39%, long-term at approximately 20%. Building depreciation means investment focus is often on land value rather than building value","inspection":"Building condition survey (建物状況調査) — agents must explain whether one has been conducted since 2018 reform. Key checks: earthquake resistance (旧耐震 vs 新耐震 — pre-1981 vs post-1981 standards, critical in seismically active Japan), structural integrity, moisture/termite damage (especially wooden buildings), roof and exterior wall condition, plumbing and electrical systems. For apartments: review management association (管理組合) records for maintenance history, repair fund adequacy, and planned major repairs. Check hazard maps for flood, landslide, and tsunami risk","mistakes":"Not considering building depreciation in long-term value, not checking earthquake resistance standards (pre-1981 buildings may have serious risk), underestimating agent commission (up to 3% + ¥60,000), not reviewing management association finances for apartments, and not understanding the Flat 35 option for 35-year rate certainty","rentVsBuy":"Japan's ultra-low mortgage rates (0.3-1.5%) make buying extremely cost-competitive with renting. Monthly mortgage payments are often lower than comparable rent in major cities. However, building depreciation (wooden houses worthless after ~22 years) means property is not the long-term store of value it is in other countries — land value is what appreciates. Buying makes financial sense after 3-5 years if you plan to stay, but expect the building component to lose value. Key factors: extremely low rates, building depreciation, and land value trends in your specific area","propertyTax":"Registration and licence tax of 0.3-1.5% at purchase. Stamp duty based on transaction value. Annual fixed asset tax (固定資産税) of 1.4% of assessed value (one-third to one-sixth of market). City planning tax (都市計画税) of up to 0.3%. Rental income at progressive rates (5-45%). Capital gains: ~39% short-term (<5 years), ~20% long-term. Housing loan deduction saves 0.7% of balance annually for 10-13 years"},
    "malaysia":{"name":"Malaysia","code":"malaysia","iso":"MY","sym":"RM","cb":"Bank Negara Malaysia","govGrants":"Stamp duty exemption for first-time buyers on properties up to RM500,000. MyFirst Home Scheme provides 100% financing guarantee for buyers earning under RM5,000/month. EPF Account 2 withdrawals for deposits and monthly payments. Housing credit guarantee through Cagamas. My Deposit scheme assists with the deposit gap. Various state-level programmes in Selangor, Penang, and Johor","refinance":"Refinancing is common after the 3-5 year lock-in period expires. Early full settlement within the lock-in incurs 2-3% penalty. After lock-in, prepayment is free. Compare rates across Public Bank, Maybank, CIMB, and RHB. Legal fees and stamp duty (0.5%) on the new loan agreement are the main costs. A rate difference of 0.5%+ typically justifies switching. Many banks offer special refinancing packages with reduced fees","investment":"Malaysian rental yields average 3-5% with strong demand in KL, Penang, and JB. Rental income is taxed at personal rates (progressive up to 30%). Allowable deductions include mortgage interest, maintenance, management fees, property tax, and fire insurance. RPGT (Real Property Gains Tax): 30% in year 1-3, 20% year 4, 15% year 5, 0% after year 5 for Malaysian citizens. Foreigners pay higher RPGT rates. Minimum purchase thresholds for foreign buyers vary by state","inspection":"For under-construction: verify developer licence, check the Housing Development Account (escrow), confirm QLASSIC score if available. Defect liability period of 24 months after vacant possession. For completed properties: engage a professional building inspector to check structural integrity, water damage, termites (common in tropical Malaysia), plumbing leaks, electrical wiring, and air conditioning systems. For strata properties: review management corporation accounts, sinking fund balance, and outstanding maintenance charges","mistakes":"Not using EPF Account 2 for the deposit, having outstanding hire purchase or personal loan payments that push DSR above the limit, not understanding freehold vs leasehold value differences, not checking CCRIS/CTOS before applying, and not comparing rates across multiple banks","rentVsBuy":"Malaysian property prices are relatively affordable compared to regional peers (Singapore, Hong Kong). With rates around 4-5% and stamp duty exemption for first-time buyers (up to RM500,000), buying becomes advantageous after 3-5 years. EPF Account 2 provides a deposit source unique to Malaysia. Key considerations: freehold vs leasehold (leasehold trades at 10-20% discount), location (KL vs secondary cities), and the lock-in period penalty if you need to sell early","propertyTax":"Stamp duty on transfer: 1-4% tiered (exempt for first-time buyers up to RM500,000). Stamp duty on loan: 0.5% (exempt for first-time buyers up to RM500,000). Quit rent and assessment (annual property tax) varies by state. RPGT: 30% (year 1-3) to 0% (after year 5) for citizens. Rental income at personal progressive rate with deductions. Legal fees follow a regulated tiered scale"},
    "mexico":{"name":"Mexico","code":"mexico","iso":"MX","sym":"$","cb":"Bank of Mexico","govGrants":"INFONAVIT credits based on employer contributions (5% of salary) at subsidised rates. FOVISSSTE for government employees. Cofinavit combines INFONAVIT with bank financing for higher-value properties. Federal subsidies for lower-income buyers purchasing affordable housing reduce the purchase price directly. CONAVI programmes target social housing. State-level programmes vary by entity","refinance":"Portabilidad (mortgage portability) allows moving to a better-rate bank under the 2014 Financial Reform. Your current bank has the right to match. No prepayment penalty on INFONAVIT loans. Bank loans may have early repayment charges in the first 2-3 years. Compare using CAT (Costo Anual Total). CONDUSEF provides an online comparison tool. Given Mexican rates of 8-13%, even a 1-2% reduction saves hundreds of thousands of pesos","investment":"Mexican rental yields average 5-8%, among the highest in Latin America. Tourist areas (Cancún, Los Cabos, Puerto Vallarta) offer strong short-term rental potential. ISR (income tax) at progressive rates on rental income with deductible expenses. Capital gains: ISR at 35% on the gain (with inflation adjustment). Foreign investors need a fideicomiso for coastal/border properties. Predial (property tax) is relatively low by international standards","inspection":"Mexican inspections should cover: structural integrity (especially seismic damage history in earthquake zones like Mexico City, Oaxaca, Guerrero), electrical wiring compliance, plumbing and drainage, roof waterproofing, finished surfaces, and municipal permits for all constructions. Verify Escritura and Registro Público records. For condominiums: review reglamento de condominio and administración accounts. In the Restricted Zone (coast/border), verify fideicomiso documentation for foreign buyers. Check for invasión (irregular occupation) risks in some areas","mistakes":"Not checking INFONAVIT eligibility first, not understanding CAT (total cost) vs tasa de interés (interest rate), buying in a poorly located development just because it qualifies for INFONAVIT, choosing a flat-rate loan over reducing-balance, and not verifying complete municipal permits for the construction","rentVsBuy":"Mexican mortgage rates of 8-13% (4-5% with INFONAVIT) make the buy-vs-rent calculation highly dependent on financing source. With INFONAVIT, buying is attractive after 3-5 years. At commercial rates, the breakeven extends to 8-12 years due to the high cost of borrowing. Rental yields of 5-8% mean rent is often cheaper than commercial mortgage payments. The key factor: INFONAVIT eligibility transforms the calculation entirely. Property appreciation of 3-8% annually in good locations helps close the gap","propertyTax":"ISAI (acquisition tax) of 2-5% by state/municipality. Gastos notariales of 4-7% (the largest cost). Annual predial (property tax) is relatively low — typically 0.1-0.3% of cadastral value. ISR on rental income at progressive rates. Capital gains ISR at 35% (with inflation adjustment and deductions). ISR exemption on primary residence sale gains up to ~700,000 UDIS every 3 years"},
    "netherlands":{"name":"Netherlands","code":"netherlands","iso":"NL","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"Startersvrijstelling: first-time buyers 18-35 exempt from 2% overdrachtsbelasting on properties up to €510,000. NHG (Nationale Hypotheek Garantie) for mortgages up to €435,000 provides rate discount (0.3-0.6%) and residual debt protection at 0.6% cost. Energiebespaarhypotheek allows borrowing up to €20,000 extra for energy improvements. Duurzaamheidslening from municipalities for insulation and solar panels at 0% interest","refinance":"Most lenders allow 10-20% penalty-free prepayment per year. Larger prepayments or full refinancing during the fixed period may incur boeterente (penalty) based on remaining term and rate differential. At each rentevaste periode expiry, switching is free. Rentemiddeling (rate averaging) with your current lender blends old and new rates without switching costs — often the simplest option. Always compare oversluiten (full switch) vs rentemiddeling before deciding","investment":"Dutch rental yields are moderate (3-5%) but the 10.4% overdrachtsbelasting on investment properties significantly increases entry costs. Box 3 taxation means the deemed return on property value is taxed at 36% (regardless of actual rental income). Rental protections in the regulated sector are strong — rent can only be raised within government-set limits. The liberalised sector (above certain rent thresholds) has more flexibility but faces increasing regulation","inspection":"A bouwkundige keuring (structural building survey) is increasingly expected by mortgage lenders and strongly recommended. The inspector assesses structural integrity, foundations, roof, facades, windows, plumbing, electrical, heating, and estimates maintenance costs for the next 10 years. For apartments: review VvE (Vereniging van Eigenaren) documents including MJOP (meerjarenonderhoudsplan — multi-year maintenance plan), reserve fund balance, monthly bijdrage (contribution), and any planned special assessments. Check for funderingsproblemen (foundation issues) in Amsterdam and other cities built on soft soil","mistakes":"Not using the first-time buyer transfer tax exemption (only for 18-35 and up to €510,000), skipping NHG for eligible purchases, not budgeting for kosten koper (4-6% from savings despite 100% financing), not getting a bouwkundige keuring, and not engaging a hypotheekadviseur (legally required independent advice)","rentVsBuy":"The Netherlands allows 100% LTV financing and has zero transfer tax for qualifying first-time buyers — making entry costs among the lowest in Europe (4-6% for kosten koper from savings). NHG protection means limited downside risk. Buying becomes advantageous after just 3-5 years considering the low entry costs and strong appreciation (5-8% annually in recent years). The hypotheekrenteaftrek (mortgage interest deduction) further reduces the effective cost of owning versus renting. Key risk: the Dutch market has experienced significant corrections historically (2008-2013)","propertyTax":"Overdrachtsbelasting: 0% for first-time buyers 18-35 (up to €510,000), 2% for other primary residences, 10.4% for investment. Hypotheekrenteaftrek (mortgage interest deduction) reduces taxable income. Eigenwoningforfait: imputed rental value of 0.35% of WOZ value is added to taxable income (partially offset by interest deduction). OZB (local property tax) varies by municipality — typically €200-600/year. Box 3 taxation for investment property based on deemed returns"},
    "new-zealand":{"name":"New Zealand","code":"new-zealand","iso":"NZ","sym":"$","cb":"Reserve Bank of New Zealand","govGrants":"First Home Grant: up to $5,000/person for new builds, $3,000 for existing homes. KiwiSaver first-home withdrawal after 3+ years of membership. First Home Loan via Kāinga Ora enables 5% deposits. Income caps (currently $95,000 individual, $150,000 combined) and regional house price caps apply. No stamp duty in New Zealand — a major cost advantage","refinance":"At each fixed-rate refix date, compare rates across all five main banks and negotiate. Break costs apply if you exit a fixed rate early — calculated based on the bank's cost of breaking the underlying swap. When switching banks, ask for cash contributions ($2,000-5,000 toward legal and switching costs). Floating-rate portions can be repaid or switched without penalty at any time","investment":"NZ rental yields average 3-5%. Bright-line test taxes gains on investment property sold within specified holding periods (currently 2 years for new builds, varies for existing). Interest deductibility on investment property loans was restricted in 2021 and is being gradually restored. Ring-fencing of rental losses means losses cannot offset other income. Healthy Homes Standards require minimum heating, insulation, ventilation, moisture, and draught stopping for rental properties","inspection":"Building inspections should cover: structure, roof, cladding, moisture testing (critical due to the leaky building crisis affecting 1994-2004 era homes), subfloor, plumbing, electrical, and insulation. LIM (Land Information Memorandum) from council reveals consents, building permits, hazard zones, rates, and special features. Check for weathertightness risk on monolithic cladding. Methamphetamine testing may be relevant for some properties. For apartments: review body corporate finances, long-term maintenance plan, and earthquake rating","mistakes":"Not joining KiwiSaver early (3-year membership required), not splitting the mortgage across multiple fixed terms, waiving the building inspection, not checking for weathertightness issues in 1994-2004 homes, and not using both KiwiSaver withdrawal and First Home Grant together","rentVsBuy":"New Zealand has zero stamp duty, making entry costs among the lowest globally (2-4% total). With current rates and prices, buying becomes advantageous after 3-5 years in most areas. Auckland's high prices extend the breakeven, while regional centres are more favourable. KiwiSaver provides a powerful deposit mechanism. The Bright-line test penalises short-term speculation but does not affect long-term owner-occupiers. Key advantage: the combination of no stamp duty, KiwiSaver withdrawal, and First Home Grant makes NZ one of the most first-buyer-friendly markets","propertyTax":"No stamp duty or transfer tax — one of very few developed countries with this advantage. Rates (local council property tax) vary by council and property value — typically $2,000-5,000/year. Bright-line test for investment properties. Rental income at marginal tax rate with limited interest deductibility. No capital gains tax on primary residence. GST of 15% applies to new residential property sold by developers"},
    "norway":{"name":"Norway","code":"norway","iso":"NO","sym":"kr","cb":"Norges Bank","govGrants":"First-time buyers exempt from 2.5% dokumentavgift. Husbanken startlån through municipalities for buyers unable to obtain conventional financing. BSU (boligsparing for ungdom) provides 20% tax deduction on savings up to kr 27,500/year for young buyers (check current status). Energy efficiency grants through Enova for heat pumps, insulation, and solar. Some municipalities offer discounted building plots","refinance":"Floating-rate mortgages (90% of market) can be repaid or switched at any time without penalty. Banks actively compete for refinancing customers. The prutesamtale (negotiation conversation) with your existing bank often yields a rate reduction without switching. If switching, the process is straightforward. Fixed-rate early termination incurs a breakage cost based on remaining term and rate differential. Compare rates annually — even 0.1-0.2% matters on Norwegian property values","investment":"Norwegian rental yields average 3-5%. Rental income is taxed at 22% (capital income rate). Expenses including mortgage interest, maintenance, insurance, and depreciation are deductible. Capital gains on investment property are taxed at 22%. Formuesskatt (wealth tax) applies to property values — currently assessed at 75% of market value for primary residence, 100% for secondary properties. Kommunale avgifter (municipal fees) are ongoing costs","inspection":"The tilstandsrapport (condition report) provided by the seller is a comprehensive document prepared by a certified building inspector (takstmann). Review it carefully for structural issues, moisture/mould, roof, electrical, plumbing, drainage, and any noted concerns. The 2022 strengthening of avhendingslova (Property Sales Act) eliminated 'as-is' sales — sellers are now liable for defects they knew or should have known about. Consider a supplementary independent inspection for older properties. Check radon levels (Norway has naturally high radon in some areas)","mistakes":"Not getting finansieringsbevis before viewings, not understanding the 5x income debt cap (student loans count), overpaying in the budrunde due to emotional bidding, not taking advantage of the dokumentavgift exemption for first-time buyers, and not negotiating rates annually with your bank","rentVsBuy":"Norway's 15% minimum deposit requirement is the main barrier to buying. Once over this hurdle, competitive floating rates and stable property appreciation (3-6% annually) make buying advantageous after 3-5 years. The dokumentavgift exemption for first-time buyers reduces entry costs to approximately 1-2%. Mortgage interest is not directly tax-deductible in Norway, but the wealth tax treatment of primary residences (75% of value vs 100% for savings) creates an implicit ownership incentive","propertyTax":"Dokumentavgift of 2.5% (exempt for first-time buyers and borettslag). Kommunal eiendomsskatt (municipal property tax) — not all municipalities charge this, but those that do charge 0.1-0.7% of assessed value. Formuesskatt (wealth tax) on net wealth above kr 1,700,000 — primary residence valued at 75% of market, secondary at 100%. Rental income at 22%. Capital gains at 22%. No specific stamp duty on the mortgage"},
    "portugal":{"name":"Portugal","code":"portugal","iso":"PT","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"First-time buyers under 35: IMT exemption on properties up to a value threshold. Bonificação de juros (interest subsidies) during high-rate periods. IHRU affordable housing programmes. Some municipalities offer reduced IMI for initial years. IRS Jovem provides reduced income tax for young workers, indirectly helping with mortgage affordability. Energy renovation incentives through Fundo Ambiental","refinance":"Early repayment penalty: 0.5% for variable-rate, 2% for fixed-rate mortgages. Transferência de crédito (bank transfer) is straightforward and regulated. Many banks offer packages to attract transfers including fee waivers. After the Euribor spike, many borrowers successfully renegotiated with existing lenders or transferred to competitors. Compare on ComparaJá.pt or through Doutor Finanças. Bonificações (linked product discounts) may change your spread if you cancel products during transfer","investment":"Portuguese rental yields average 4-7%, with Lisbon at the lower end and Algarve/Porto offering strong returns. The AL (Alojamento Local) short-term rental licence faces increasing restrictions in some municipalities. Rental income is taxed at progressive rates or 28% flat rate for residents. Non-resident landlords pay 25% flat rate. Capital gains: 50% of the gain is taxed at progressive rates for residents (full gain at 28% for non-residents). IMI exemption may apply for initial years. Golden Visa ended for real estate but NHR (Non-Habitual Resident) tax regime has been reformed — check current status","inspection":"Portuguese buildings can be old (especially in Lisbon and Porto centres), making inspection critical. Check: structural integrity (especially for pre-earthquake-standard buildings — 1755 and later building codes), moisture and damp (common in older Portuguese buildings), electrical and plumbing compliance, roof condition (flat roofs and terracotta tiles), asbestos in pre-1990s buildings. Obtain and verify: Caderneta Predial (property booklet from tax authority), Certidão do Registo Predial (land registry certificate), licença de utilização (usage licence), and certificado energético (energy certificate, mandatory)","mistakes":"Not comparing rates across multiple banks, signing up for expensive vinculações without evaluating value, choosing variable rate without understanding Euribor risk, not negotiating the spread aggressively, and not checking planning compliance for renovated properties","rentVsBuy":"Portugal's relatively affordable property prices and favourable tax treatment for primary residences make buying attractive. IMT exemption for young first-time buyers reduces entry costs. At current rates (3-5% fixed), buying becomes advantageous after 4-7 years in most areas. Lisbon and Porto prices have risen significantly in recent years, extending the breakeven in premium locations. Key consideration: the Euribor-linked variable rate that many Portuguese borrowers hold makes monthly costs sensitive to ECB decisions","propertyTax":"IMT: 0-8% sliding scale (exempt for young FTBs up to threshold). Imposto de Selo of 0.8% on purchase + 0.6% on loan. Annual IMI of 0.3-0.8% of taxable value. Rental income at progressive rates or 28% flat. Capital gains: 50% of gain at progressive rates for residents. AIMI (additional to IMI) on properties valued above €600,000. IMI exemption for primary residence may apply for initial 3 years"},
    "saudi-arabia":{"name":"Saudi Arabia","code":"saudi-arabia","iso":"SA","sym":"ر.س","cb":"Saudi Central Bank","govGrants":"Sakani provides comprehensive housing support: free land allocation, REDF profit-free financing up to SAR 500,000, subsidised mortgage products through partner banks, and ready-built affordable homes. The programme targets Saudi nationals and has supported hundreds of thousands of families. Eligibility depends on marital status, age, and property ownership history. REDF guarantee programmes enable higher LTV through commercial banks","refinance":"SAMA allows early settlement with a penalty of up to 3 months' profit. The salary assignment (tahweel) requirement adds administrative complexity to switching banks — your employer must re-assign your salary to the new bank. Compare offers from SNB, Al Rajhi, Riyad Bank, and others. The REDF subsidy component cannot be transferred, but the commercial portion can potentially be refinanced separately","investment":"Saudi rental yields average 5-8%, supported by Vision 2030 growth and population increases. Rental income is not taxed for Saudi individuals (no personal income tax). Zakat at 2.5% applies to net wealth. RETT of 5% on transactions. The market is heavily influenced by government mega-projects (NEOM, The Line, Diriyah Gate) which create new demand centres. Foreign investment is increasingly welcome under Vision 2030 reforms","inspection":"For new builds: verify compliance with Saudi Building Code (SBC), check construction permits from the municipality. For off-plan: verify developer registration with the Wafi platform, confirm escrow account arrangements, review delivery timeline and penalty clauses. For existing properties: structural integrity, electrical compliance, plumbing and water systems, air conditioning (critical in Saudi climate), and any signs of foundation settlement. Use the Ejar platform to verify rental documentation for investment properties","mistakes":"Not checking Sakani/REDF eligibility first, not understanding the salary assignment (tahweel) requirement, rushing into off-plan without verifying developer Wafi registration, not comparing across multiple banks for the commercial portion, and not understanding the difference between Murabaha and Ijara financing structures","rentVsBuy":"With REDF profit-free financing, Saudi nationals can access some of the cheapest housing finance in the world — making buying almost always advantageous for eligible citizens after 2-3 years. For non-REDF buyers using commercial financing, the breakeven extends to 5-8 years. No income tax means rental payments come from gross income, but so do mortgage payments — the comparison is direct. Vision 2030 targets have driven home ownership from ~47% to over 60%, reflecting the government's strong push toward ownership","propertyTax":"RETT of 5% on property transactions (replaced 15% VAT in 2020). No annual property tax for residential property. No personal income tax. Zakat at 2.5% on net wealth (for Muslim citizens). White Land Tax of 2.5% on undeveloped urban land above 10,000 sqm (to encourage development). Municipal fees for commercial properties. No capital gains tax on residential property for individuals"},
    "singapore":{"name":"Singapore","code":"singapore","iso":"SG","sym":"$","cb":"Monetary Authority of Singapore","govGrants":"CPF Housing Grants: up to $80,000 Enhanced CPF Housing Grant for eligible first-time HDB resale buyers. BTO flats at 20-30% below market value. Step-Up Grant of $15,000 for second-timer families. Proximity Housing Grant of $20,000 for living near parents. CPF OA savings and monthly contributions for deposit and payments. EC (Executive Condominium) straddles HDB and private markets with initial subsidies","refinance":"For bank loans: refinance every 2-3 years when the lock-in expires to get new promotional rates. Legal fees of $2,000-3,000 apply. HDB loan to bank: possible but you lose penalty-free prepayment and the guaranteed 2.6% rate — and cannot switch back. Bank to bank: straightforward, many banks offer incentives for refinancing customers. Partial prepayment during lock-in incurs 1.5% penalty on amount repaid","investment":"Singapore rental yields average 3-4% for condominiums. ABSD makes investment expensive: 20% for second property (citizens), 30% for third+, 60% for foreigners. Rental income is taxed at personal rates (0-22%). Property tax is progressive: owner-occupier rates of 0-16%, non-owner-occupier rates of 12-36%. Capital gains are not taxed in Singapore (no CGT). SSD (Seller's Stamp Duty) applies if sold within 3 years of purchase","inspection":"For resale HDB/private: engage a professional inspector for structural defects, water leaks, damp, electrical, plumbing, and flooring. For new condominiums: defect inspection during the Defects Liability Period (typically 12 months). Check for hacking (unauthorized removal of walls). Review the building's sinking fund and maintenance history. For condominiums: check en-bloc sale potential, remaining lease (critical for older 99-year leasehold properties), and any major upgrading works planned. MCST (Management Corporation Strata Title) minutes may reveal ongoing issues","mistakes":"Not understanding CPF accrued interest (2.5% compounding reduces net sale proceeds), not comparing HDB loan vs bank loan properly, overextending with a bank loan when HDB is more suitable, not factoring in renovation costs ($30,000-80,000 for HDB), and not checking remaining lease for older leasehold properties","rentVsBuy":"Singapore's HDB system makes buying uniquely accessible for citizens — BTO flats at 20-30% below market with CPF grants of up to $80,000. For HDB, buying is almost always superior to renting after 3-5 years. For private property, high ABSD and prices mean buying makes sense only for longer stays (7+ years). CPF usage reduces cash outflow but the 2.5% accrued interest is a hidden cost. Key insight: the dual market means the analysis is completely different for HDB (strongly favours buying) versus private (depends on duration and ABSD status)","propertyTax":"BSD of 1-6% (all buyers). ABSD: 0% first property (citizen HDB), 20% second, 30% third+, 60% foreigners. SSD if sold within 3 years. Annual property tax: 0-16% for owner-occupied (progressive on annual value), 12-36% for non-owner. No capital gains tax. Rental income at personal rates (0-22%). CPF accrued interest (2.5%) on amounts used for property — refunded to CPF on sale"},
    "south-africa":{"name":"South Africa","code":"south-africa","iso":"ZA","sym":"R","cb":"South African Reserve Bank","govGrants":"FLISP provides R30,001-R130,505 subsidy for first-time buyers earning R3,501-R22,000/month. Transfer duty exemption below R1,100,000. National Housing Finance Corporation programmes expand finance access. Various provincial housing programmes. Social housing options in some municipalities for qualifying households","refinance":"Variable-rate home loans have zero prepayment penalty — switch at any time. Use a bond originator (BetterBond, ooba) to compare across all banks. If your financial profile has improved (higher income, better credit, lower LTV from appreciation), you likely qualify for a better rate. Negotiate with your current bank first — they often match competing offers to avoid losing you. The process is similar to a new bond application","investment":"South African rental yields average 5-8%, among the highest in comparable markets. Rental income is taxed at personal rates (progressive up to 45%). Deductible expenses: bond interest, rates, levies, insurance, repairs, management fees. Capital gains: 40% inclusion rate for individuals at marginal tax rate (effective rate of 7.2-18%). Primary residence exclusion of R2,000,000 on CGT. Sectional title levies and special levies are ongoing costs for apartment investors","inspection":"Mandatory compliance certificates (seller's responsibility): electrical COC, plumbing COC, gas COC (if applicable), beetle/wood borer, and electric fence. Buyers should additionally commission: structural inspection (check for cracks, settlement, damp, roof condition), assessment of outbuildings and boundary walls, verification of municipal approved plans (check for unapproved additions), and water damage assessment. For sectional title: review body corporate financials, levy history, insurance cover, and conduct rules","mistakes":"Not using a bond originator, having a poor credit record without addressing it first, not understanding that transfer costs must come from savings even with 100% bond, accepting the bank's first rate without negotiating, and not checking for unapproved building works which affect value and resale","rentVsBuy":"South Africa's 100% bond availability means zero deposit buying is genuinely possible. With transfer duty exemption below R1,100,000, entry costs can be as low as 2-3% (bond registration and transfer fees). Buying becomes advantageous after 3-5 years in most areas. At current prime-linked rates, monthly bond repayments are often comparable to rent for similar properties. Key factors: credit record quality (determines your rate), property location (affects appreciation), and the rate cycle (SARB decisions affect all variable-rate bondholders simultaneously)","propertyTax":"Transfer duty: 0% up to R1,100,000, then 3-13% sliding scale. Annual rates (municipal property tax) vary by municipality. Rental income at personal progressive rate (up to 45%). Capital gains: 40% inclusion at marginal rate (R2,000,000 primary residence exclusion). Levies for sectional title properties. No VAT on residential resale between individuals (14% VAT applies to developer sales of new stock)"},
    "south-korea":{"name":"South Korea","code":"south-korea","iso":"KR","sym":"₩","cb":"Bank of Korea","govGrants":"Bogeumjari Loan through HF offers long-term fixed rates for owner-occupiers. Didimdol Loan provides subsidised rates for first-time buyers and newlyweds. Youth programmes (under 34-39) offer reduced rates. Special newlywed programmes (신혼부부 특례) provide enhanced terms. Government periodically adjusts LTV/DTI limits to manage market — relaxations benefit buyers","refinance":"대환대출 (loan conversion) has become easier. Early repayment penalty of 1-1.5% within 3 years, reducing to zero after 3 years. Compare using Banksalad or Finda. Government-backed loan refinancing programmes exist during rate change periods. The 3-year penalty cliff makes timing important — refinance just after the penalty expires for maximum savings","investment":"Korean rental yields average 2-4% in Seoul, higher in secondary cities. The jeonse system means many investment properties are rented on a deposit basis rather than monthly rent. Comprehensive real estate tax (종합부동산세) applies to high-value holdings. Capital gains tax: 6-45% (plus additional 20-30% surcharge for multi-property owners in speculative zones). Acquisition tax of 1-12% depending on property count and value. Rental income tax at progressive rates","inspection":"Check the 등기부등본 (property registry) for ownership, mortgages, liens, and encumbrances. Verify 건축물대장 (building ledger) for legal floor area, use, and compliance. Inspect for: structural issues, moisture and mould (common in Korean apartment complexes), heating system (ondol underfloor heating), window insulation (important for Korean winters), and elevator condition. For apartments: review 관리비 (management fee) breakdown, 장기수선충당금 (long-term maintenance fund) balance, and any planned major repairs. Check earthquake resistance rating","mistakes":"Not understanding zone-based LTV/DTI restrictions, not checking government-backed loan eligibility first, not understanding DSR limits, not timing purchases around regulation changes, and not understanding the jeonse vs wolse implications for market dynamics","rentVsBuy":"Korea's market is unique due to the jeonse (전세) system — large deposit rental that ties up significant capital. In speculative zones (Gangnam), high deposit requirements (60-80%) make buying very capital-intensive. In non-speculative areas with 60-70% LTV, buying is more accessible and advantageous after 4-7 years. Government-backed products (Bogeumjari, Didimdol) significantly improve the calculation. Key consideration: regulation changes affect both LTV limits and tax treatment, making timing important","propertyTax":"Acquisition tax: 1-3.5% (up to 12% for multi-property in speculative zones). Registration tax and education tax: ~0.4%. Annual property tax: 0.1-0.4% of assessed value. Comprehensive real estate tax (종합부동산세) for holdings above thresholds. Capital gains: 6-45% plus surcharges for multi-owners. Rental income at progressive rates. Jeonse deposit income is not directly taxed but affects wealth calculations"},
    "spain":{"name":"Spain","code":"spain","iso":"ES","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","govGrants":"Limited national schemes — autonomous communities offer various programmes. Some regions reduce ITP for first-time buyers, young buyers (under 32-35), large families, or rural area purchases. ICO has introduced guarantee programmes for under-35 first-time buyers. Plan de Vivienda provides subsidies in certain regions. Andalucía, Valencia, and Madrid offer specific tax reductions for qualifying buyers. Check your autonomous community's current programmes","refinance":"Novación (renegotiation with current bank) is cheapest. Subrogación (transfer to new bank) is handled by the new bank. Cancelación y nueva constitución is most flexible but most expensive. Early repayment caps: 0.25% for variable, 2% for fixed (reducing over term). After the Euribor spike, many borrowers converted variable to fixed through novación or subrogación. The 2019 Mortgage Law protections apply to all refinancing options","investment":"Spanish rental yields average 4-7% with strong tourist demand in coastal and island areas. ITP on investment properties is 6-10%. Annual IBI (property tax) applies. Rental income is taxed at 19-24% for non-residents, progressive rates for residents (with 60% reduction for long-term residential rentals). Capital gains at 19-28% progressive. Plusvalía municipal (local capital gains tax) applies on sale. Tourist rental licences are increasingly restricted in popular areas","inspection":"Key checks: nota simple from the Registro de la Propiedad (ownership, charges, encumbrances), ITE (Inspección Técnica de Edificios) for buildings over 50 years, Certificado de Eficiencia Energética (mandatory for sale), urban planning compliance (vital — check for illegal constructions, particularly in rural and coastal areas), cédula de habitabilidad (habitability certificate) in some autonomous communities. Physical inspection for structural issues, moisture, roof, plumbing, electrical. For coastal properties: check for saline damage and proximity to costal domain boundaries","mistakes":"Underestimating buying costs (10-15%), not understanding regional ITP differences (6-10%), not checking urban planning compliance (illegal constructions are a serious issue), accepting vinculaciones without evaluating their true cost, and not using the 10-day FEIN reflection period to compare offers","rentVsBuy":"Spain offers affordable property by Western European standards, especially outside Madrid and Barcelona. With ITP of 6-10% and notary costs, entry is expensive. Buying becomes advantageous after 6-10 years in most areas. In tourist zones with strong rental demand, investment buying can work sooner. The key factor is the autonomous community's tax rates — buying in Madrid (6% ITP) is significantly cheaper than Catalonia (10%). Under the 2019 law, most mortgage costs are now paid by the bank, reducing the buyer's burden","propertyTax":"ITP: 6-10% for resale (by autonomous community), or IVA 10% + AJD 0.5-1.5% for new. Under 2019 law, bank pays AJD on the mortgage, notary, and registry fees. Annual IBI (property tax) of 0.4-1.1% of cadastral value. Rental income: progressive for residents (60% reduction for long-term), 19-24% for non-residents. Capital gains: 19-28% progressive. Plusvalía municipal on sale. Patrimonio (wealth tax) in some communities"},
    "sweden":{"name":"Sweden","code":"sweden","iso":"SE","sym":"kr","cb":"Sveriges Riksbank","govGrants":"Limited direct buyer subsidies in Sweden. ROT-avdrag provides 30% tax deduction on labour costs for home renovations (up to kr 50,000/person/year). Some municipalities offer discounted building land. The amorteringskrav (mandatory amortisation) rules structure the market — not a subsidy but fundamentally shapes buying behaviour. Energy efficiency grants through Boverket for certain upgrades","refinance":"At each ränteperiod expiry, switching banks is free and common. Swedish banks expect negotiation — always compare using Compricer.se before your rate resets. Breaking a fixed rate early incurs ränteskillnadsersättning (interest differential compensation). Existing pantbrev (mortgage certificates) transfer to the new bank at zero cost — a significant saving (otherwise 2% registration). The prutesamtale (rate negotiation) with your current bank often yields a reduction without switching","investment":"Swedish rental yields average 2-4% for apartments, higher for houses in some regions. Rental income from a subletting a bostadsrätt requires förening (cooperative) approval and rent is regulated. Capital gains tax of 22% on profit from property sale. ROT-avdrag on renovation can improve property value tax-efficiently. Uthyrning av privatbostad (private rental) allows tax-free rental income up to kr 40,000/year for primary residence subletting, with 20% tax above that","inspection":"Swedish law places a strong undersökningsplikt (investigation obligation) on the buyer — defects discoverable through reasonable inspection cannot be claimed against the seller later. A professional besiktning (building inspection) is therefore essential and standard practice. The inspector assesses structure, roof, facade, moisture (critical in Swedish climate), drainage, plumbing, electrical, ventilation, and energy efficiency. For bostadsrätter: review the förening's årsredovisning (annual report) for cooperative debt levels, planned maintenance, and financial health — the förening's debt effectively adds to your personal debt burden","mistakes":"Not understanding the amorteringskrav (mandatory amortisation of 1-3%/year depending on LTV and income multiple), not investigating the bostadsrättsförening's finances, overpaying in the budgivning, not negotiating rates at every renewal, and not understanding that pantbrev transfer free (saving 2% on existing amounts)","rentVsBuy":"Sweden's 85% LTV cap requires 15% deposit — the main barrier. Once saved, competitive rates and the amorteringskrav (forced equity building) make buying advantageous after 4-6 years. For bostadsrätter, the monthly avgift must be added to mortgage costs in the comparison. Stockholm prices are high, making renting competitive for shorter stays. Key insight: the amorteringskrav means you are building equity from day one (1-3%/year forced repayment), which tips the balance toward buying for longer stays","propertyTax":"Lagfart of 1.5% for houses (not bostadsrätter). Pantbrev of 2% on new amounts (existing transfer free). No stamp duty. Annual kommunal fastighetsavgift (property fee) capped at approximately kr 9,100 for houses. Capital gains at 22%. Rental income: kr 40,000 tax-free for primary residence subletting, 20% above that. For bostadsrätter: monthly avgift covers building costs but is not tax-deductible. Uppskov (capital gains deferral) available when buying a replacement property"},
    "switzerland":{"name":"Switzerland","code":"switzerland","iso":"CH","sym":"CHF","cb":"Swiss National Bank","govGrants":"Pillar 2 and Pillar 3a pension funds can be used toward the minimum 20% equity (at least 10% must be non-pension). Wohneigentumsförderung allows pension fund withdrawal or pledge for residential property. Some cantons offer subsidised construction loans or reduced-rate mortgages for families. Gemeinnütziger Wohnungsbau (cooperative housing) receives federal support. Canton-specific programmes vary significantly","refinance":"At Festhypothek expiry, switching is free — compare across banks, insurance companies, and pension funds using MoneyPark. Breaking early incurs Vorfälligkeitsentschädigung that can be very substantial (one-sided calculation based on bank's opportunity cost). Forward-Hypotheken allow locking in rates up to 24 months early. Consider indirect amortisation through Pillar 3a as a refinancing strategy — redirecting payments from direct to indirect amortisation can be tax-efficient","investment":"Swiss investment property yields average 3-5%. Lex Koller restrictions apply to foreign buyers. Eigenmietwert taxation means all owned property generates taxable imputed income. Rental income is taxed as regular income minus deductible expenses. Capital gains on property are taxed by the canton (Grundstückgewinnsteuer) at declining rates with holding period — short-term sales are heavily taxed. Liegenschaftssteuer (property tax) varies by canton. Investment property mortgage rates are typically 0.2-0.5% higher than residential","inspection":"Swiss construction quality is generally high but inspections are still recommended, particularly for older properties. Check: structural integrity, Minergie energy rating if applicable, heating system efficiency, moisture and insulation (important in Alpine climate), roof condition, common area maintenance for Stockwerkeigentum (condominiums). Review Gebäudeversicherung (building insurance) details. For condominiums: review Stockwerkeigentümergemeinschaft regulations, Erneuerungsfonds (renovation fund) balance, and planned works","mistakes":"Not understanding the Tragbarkeit (affordability) test at 5% imputed rate, underestimating the Eigenmietwert tax impact of ownership, not comparing across enough lenders (MoneyPark accesses 100+), breaking a Festhypothek early (enormous penalty), and not using Pillar 3a for indirect amortisation","rentVsBuy":"Switzerland's unique tax system (Eigenmietwert taxation + mortgage interest deductibility) makes the buy-vs-rent calculation unlike any other country. Owning is not always clearly better than renting due to the imputed rental income tax. The Tragbarkeit test at 5% also limits borrowing capacity, meaning many residents who could afford actual payments fail the stress test. Buying typically becomes advantageous after 5-10 years considering the high deposit requirement (20%), transaction costs (1-5% depending on canton), and the tax implications. In Zurich and Geneva with very high prices, the breakeven extends further","propertyTax":"Handänderungssteuer: 0-3.3% depending on canton. Notary fees: 0.1-0.5%. Grundbuch: 0.1-0.3%. Annual Liegenschaftssteuer varies by canton. Eigenmietwert (imputed rental value) added to taxable income — the unique Swiss system. Mortgage interest deductible against Eigenmietwert. Grundstückgewinnsteuer (property capital gains tax) at cantonal rates declining with holding period. Vermögenssteuer (wealth tax) includes property value in net worth assessment"},
    "thailand":{"name":"Thailand","code":"thailand","iso":"TH","sym":"฿","cb":"Bank of Thailand","govGrants":"GH Bank provides below-market rates for Thai nationals. Government periodically reduces transfer and mortgage registration fees to 0.01% (from 2% and 1% respectively). First-time buyer programmes through GH Bank offer enhanced terms. Social Security Office housing loan programme for insured workers. Baan Eur-Arthorn provides affordable housing through the National Housing Authority. Check current fee reduction campaign status","refinance":"Refinancing (รีไฟแนนซ์) is common after the 1-3 year promotional period expires and the rate reverts to a higher MRR-based rate. Early repayment penalty of 2-3% applies within the lock-in period (typically 3-5 years). After lock-in, prepayment and refinancing are free. Banks offer competitive promotional rates to attract refinancing customers. Compare across GH Bank, Bangkok Bank, KBank, and SCB","investment":"Thai rental yields average 4-7%, with Bangkok and resort areas (Phuket, Pattaya, Hua Hin) offering strong returns. Foreigners can only own condo freehold (49% foreign quota). Rental income is taxed at progressive rates (5-35%) for Thai residents. Withholding tax of 5-35% applies to non-residents. Specific business tax of 3.3% if property is sold within 5 years. Condominium common area fees of ฿2,000-8,000/month. Sinking fund is a one-time cost at purchase","inspection":"For condominiums: verify the 49% foreign ownership quota, review juristic person financial status, check common area maintenance, inspect for water leaks (common in Thai construction), verify fire safety systems. For houses: structural integrity, roof waterproofing, termite damage (common in tropical Thailand), flooding risk (check historical flood maps), electrical wiring, and plumbing. For all properties: verify the Chanote (title deed) at the Land Department, confirm no encumbrances, and check for any pending legal issues. Engage an independent property lawyer — not the agent's recommended lawyer","mistakes":"Not checking GH Bank first, not understanding the promotional vs reversion rate, not verifying the Chanote title deed at the Land Department, buying a condominium without checking the 49% foreign quota, and not timing purchases during government fee reduction campaigns","rentVsBuy":"With GH Bank subsidised rates, Thai nationals can access affordable financing making buying advantageous after 3-5 years. For commercial bank borrowers at higher rates, the breakeven extends to 6-9 years. Foreigners face restrictions (condo only) and limited financing options, making the calculation different — many foreign buyers purchase cash. Government fee reduction campaigns can save 3-4% in transaction costs, significantly improving the buying case. Key factors: GH Bank eligibility, fee reduction campaign timing, and the promotional-to-reversion rate transition","propertyTax":"Transfer fee: 2% (typically split). Stamp duty: 0.5%. Specific business tax: 3.3% if sold within 5 years. Withholding tax on seller. Annual land and building tax: 0.02-0.1% for residential (progressive, with exemptions for primary residence under ฿50 million). Rental income at progressive rates (5-35%). Common area fees for condominiums. All fees may be reduced during government campaigns"},
    "uae":{"name":"UAE","code":"uae","iso":"AE","sym":"د.إ","cb":"Central Bank of the UAE","govGrants":"No traditional buyer subsidies (zero income tax environment). Golden Visa (10-year residency for AED 2M+ property investment). 2-year property visa for AED 750,000+. Mohammed bin Rashid Housing Establishment provides housing for UAE nationals. Abu Dhabi Housing Authority for Emirati citizens. Developer payment plans (1-3% per month during construction) provide alternative financing paths not available in most markets","refinance":"Early settlement fee: 1% of outstanding balance (or 3 months' interest, whichever is lower) for variable-rate. Fixed-rate portions may have higher penalties. Banks actively compete for refinancing business with promotional rates and fee waivers. The process takes 4-8 weeks including NOC, new valuation, and transfer at DLD/ADMA. Some banks offer rate-match guarantees. Use a mortgage broker (Holo, Huspy) to compare all offers","investment":"UAE rental yields average 5-8%, among the highest globally for comparable markets. Zero income tax means gross yield equals net yield (before service charges). DLD registration of 4% is the main transaction cost. Service charges of AED 15-30/sqft/year significantly affect net returns. Capital gains are not taxed. The market is cyclical with strong off-plan activity. Golden Visa at AED 2M+ adds residency value. RERA rental index determines permissible rent increases for existing tenancies","inspection":"For ready properties: professional snagging inspection covering structural, finishes, electrical, plumbing, AC, and appliances. For off-plan: verify developer's RERA registration and escrow account, review payment plan and completion timeline, check the SPA (Sale and Purchase Agreement) carefully. For all: verify DEWA connection, check service charges with the community management company, review DLD title deed, and confirm no outstanding fees. For older properties: AC efficiency, waterproofing (especially in high-rise towers), and fire safety compliance are critical in the UAE climate","mistakes":"Not understanding ABSD-equivalent (higher deposit for subsequent properties), buying off-plan without verifying RERA registration, not factoring in service charges (AED 15-30/sqft/year), not comparing Islamic vs conventional products, and not using a mortgage broker to access all bank offers","rentVsBuy":"UAE's zero income tax and high rental yields (5-8%) make property investment attractive. For owner-occupiers, buying becomes advantageous after 4-7 years considering the 4% DLD transfer fee and 7-9% total buying costs. The Golden Visa (AED 2M+) adds immigration value that is not captured in pure financial analysis. Off-plan with developer payment plans provides an alternative entry with lower upfront capital. Key factors: the DLD 4% fee, service charges, expected holding period, and whether Golden Visa residency is valuable to you","propertyTax":"DLD transfer fee: 4% in Dubai (split), 2% in Abu Dhabi. No annual property tax on residential. No income tax on rental income. No capital gains tax. Service charges: AED 15-30/sqft/year. DEWA connection deposits. Oqood registration: 4% for off-plan. Mortgage registration: 0.25% + AED 290. No wealth tax, inheritance tax, or stamp duty beyond DLD. This zero-tax environment is a major draw for international investors"},
    "united-kingdom":{"name":"United Kingdom","code":"united-kingdom","iso":"GB","sym":"£","cb":"Bank of England","govGrants":"SDLT relief: 0% up to £425,000 for first-time buyers (properties up to £625,000). Lifetime ISA: 25% government bonus on savings up to £4,000/year (ages 18-39). Shared Ownership: buy 25-75% and rent the rest, with staircasing option. Mortgage Guarantee Scheme enables 95% LTV. First Homes provides 30%+ discount on new builds. Scottish, Welsh, and NI programmes have separate schemes","refinance":"Remortgaging or product transfer every 2-5 years is essential — staying on the SVR costs 2-4% more than the best deals. Product transfer (staying with your lender on a new rate) is simplest and avoids legal fees. Full remortgage to a new lender gives access to the whole market but costs £500-1,500 in legal fees. ERCs apply only during the deal period — once it ends, switching is penalty-free. Start shopping 3-6 months before your deal expires. Most lenders allow 10% overpayment per year without ERCs","investment":"UK rental yields average 4-7% depending on location (north of England typically higher than London). Buy-to-let mortgage rates are higher than residential (typically 1-2% more). Additional 3% SDLT surcharge on second properties. Rental income at marginal rate with mortgage interest relief capped at basic rate (20%). Capital gains at 18% (basic rate) or 24% (higher rate) with £3,000 annual allowance. Section 24 tax changes have significantly impacted buy-to-let profitability","inspection":"Three survey levels: Level 1 (Condition Report — basic, for new/conventional homes), Level 2 (HomeBuyer Report — the standard for most purchases, including valuation and condition), Level 3 (Building Survey — full structural, for older, larger, or unusual properties). In Scotland, the seller provides a Home Report. UK-specific concerns: flood risk (check Environment Agency maps), subsidence (particularly in clay soil areas of London and South East), Japanese knotweed (can affect mortgageability), radon (elevated in Devon, Cornwall, Somerset), EPC rating (minimum E for rental, moving to C), and listed building restrictions","mistakes":"Not using a whole-of-market broker, staying on SVR after deal expires, not understanding leasehold implications (ground rent, service charges, lease extension costs), underestimating how fast you need to move in competitive markets, and not getting a proper survey (Level 2 minimum for any property over 10 years old)","rentVsBuy":"UK buying costs are moderate by European standards — SDLT relief for first-time buyers (0% up to £425,000) and relatively low legal costs mean entry barriers are manageable. Buying becomes advantageous after 4-6 years in most areas outside London. In London, high prices extend the breakeven to 6-10+ years. The key UK dynamic is the regular remortgaging cycle (every 2-5 years), which means borrowers actively manage their rate rather than locking in for decades. Strong long-term appreciation (3-5% nationally) supports the buying case for longer stays","propertyTax":"SDLT: 0% up to £250,000 (FTBs: 0% up to £425,000), 5% on £250,001-£925,000, 10% on £925,001-£1.5M, 12% above. Additional 3% on second properties. Scotland: LBTT with different bands. Wales: LTT. Council tax: £1,000-5,000+/year depending on band and area. Rental income at marginal rate. Capital gains: 18%/24% with £3,000 allowance. Mortgage interest relief at basic rate only for buy-to-let. 2% non-resident SDLT surcharge"},
    "united-states":{"name":"United States","code":"united-states","iso":"US","sym":"$","cb":"Federal Reserve","govGrants":"FHA loans: 3.5% down with government insurance. VA loans: 0% down for military, no PMI. USDA loans: 0% down for rural areas. Fannie Mae HomeReady and Freddie Mac Home Possible: 3% down for lower-income buyers. State HFA down payment assistance and closing cost grants. Mortgage interest deduction on loans up to $750,000. First-time homebuyer penalty-free IRA withdrawal of $10,000. Various city and county first-time buyer programmes","refinance":"No prepayment penalty on most US mortgages. Rate-and-term refinance replaces your loan at a better rate. Cash-out refinance accesses equity (up to 80% LTV). FHA/VA/USDA streamline refinances simplify the process with reduced documentation and no appraisal. Closing costs of 2-5% apply, so calculate the break-even point (months of savings to recoup costs). With 30-year fixed rates, refinancing when rates drop even 0.5-0.75% can save tens of thousands","investment":"US rental yields average 4-8% depending on market. Rental income at marginal tax rate minus deductible expenses (mortgage interest, depreciation over 27.5 years, property tax, insurance, management, repairs). 1031 exchange allows deferring capital gains by reinvesting in like-kind property. Capital gains: 0-20% for long-term (over 1 year). FIRPTA requires 15% withholding when foreigners sell US property. Property taxes of 0.5-2.5% annually are a significant ongoing cost varying dramatically by state","inspection":"Professional inspection covers structure, foundation, roof, plumbing, electrical, HVAC, insulation, windows/doors, and exterior grading. Most contracts include an inspection contingency. Additional specialised inspections: radon testing (especially in Midwest and Northeast), termite/pest, mould, lead paint (pre-1978 homes), asbestos, well water quality, septic system, and sewer scope. The appraisal (required by lender) is separate from the inspection and may flag additional issues. For condos: review HOA financials, reserve fund, CC&Rs, and any pending litigation","mistakes":"Not shopping multiple lenders (rate spreads of 0.5-1.0% cost tens of thousands), not understanding PMI costs on conventional loans above 80% LTV, waiving inspection contingency, not getting pre-approved before house hunting, and not considering 15-year fixed if you can afford the payments","rentVsBuy":"The US offers extraordinary financing options — VA loans at 0% down with no PMI, FHA at 3.5%, and conventional at 3-5%. The mortgage interest deduction provides tax benefits for itemising homeowners. Buying becomes advantageous after 3-5 years in most markets considering closing costs of 3-6%. In high-cost coastal markets (SF, NYC), the breakeven extends to 5-8+ years. Property taxes (0.5-2.5% annually) are a major ongoing cost that varies dramatically by state — factor these into the comparison. The 30-year fixed rate provides unmatched payment certainty","propertyTax":"Transfer taxes vary by state (0-2.5%). Title insurance of 0.5-1%. Annual property taxes of 0.5-2.5% of assessed value (the largest ongoing cost of ownership, varying dramatically by state). Mortgage interest deductible on loans up to $750,000 (if itemising). Capital gains: 0-20% long-term, plus 3.8% net investment income tax for high earners. Primary residence exclusion of $250,000 ($500,000 married). SALT deduction capped at $10,000"},
}

def gen_blog(code, d, slug, title_tmpl, meta_desc, category, content_fn, related):
    iso = d["iso"].lower()
    title = title_tmpl.format(**d)
    body = content_fn(code, d, iso)
    related_yaml = "\n".join(f"  - {r}" for r in related)
    return f"""---
title: "{title}"
country: "{code}"
slug: "{slug}"
category: "{category}"
metaTitle: "{title} | URMortgage"
metaDescription: "{meta_desc.format(**d)}"
primaryKeyword: "{slug.replace('-', ' ')} {d['name']}"
secondaryKeywords:
  - "{d['name']} property advice"
  - "{slug.replace('-', ' ')} tips"
publishDate: 2026-07-10
lastUpdated: 2026-08-18
wordCount: 1200
relatedPosts:
{related_yaml}
published: true
---

{body}
"""

# ========== TOPIC GENERATORS ==========

def body_gov_grants(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Government Grants and Schemes for Property Buyers in {d['name']}

Buying property is one of the largest financial commitments you will make. Government programmes in {d['name']} can significantly reduce costs through grants, tax exemptions, subsidised financing, and other support mechanisms. This guide covers every major programme available in 2026.

## Current Programmes and Eligibility

{d['govGrants']}

## How to Apply

Eligibility criteria, application windows, and benefit amounts change periodically. Always verify current terms directly with the administering body before making purchase decisions based on expected support. Apply for relevant programmes early in your buying process — some have limited funding or processing times that require advance planning.

## Combining Multiple Benefits

In {d['name']}, it is often possible to combine multiple government programmes. For example, first-time buyer tax relief can be combined with subsidised financing or grants. Calculate the total benefit package before finalising your budget — the combined value can be substantial.

## Tax Benefits of Property Ownership

Beyond direct grants and subsidies, property ownership in {d['name']} often comes with ongoing tax advantages. These may include deductions on mortgage interest, reduced rates on primary residences, or exemptions on capital gains. Our [property tax guide](/{code}/blog/property-tax-guide/) covers all applicable taxes in detail.

## Next Steps

Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to see how government support affects your repayments. Read our [first home buyer guide](/{code}/blog/first-home-buyer-guide/) for the complete buying process, or browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/) for quick answers.
"""

def body_refinancing(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## When and How to Refinance Your Mortgage in {d['name']}

If your mortgage is more than a couple of years old, you may be paying more than necessary. Refinancing — switching to a better rate, different product, or new lender — can save you thousands over the remaining term. This guide explains when refinancing makes sense and how to do it in {d['name']}.

## When Refinancing Makes Sense

Refinancing typically makes sense when market rates have dropped significantly below your current rate, your financial profile has improved (higher income, better credit score, lower LTV through property appreciation), you want to switch from variable to fixed rate (or vice versa), you want to access equity for renovations or other purposes, or you want to change your loan term.

## How Refinancing Works in {d['name']}

{d['refinance']}

## Calculating the Break-Even Point

The break-even point is how long the monthly savings take to exceed the costs of switching. Divide total switching costs by monthly savings to get the number of months. If you plan to stay longer than the break-even point, refinancing makes financial sense. Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to model the savings.

## When NOT to Refinance

Refinancing does not always make sense. If the rate difference is small and switching costs are high, you may be better staying. If you are close to paying off the mortgage, the savings period is too short. If your property has decreased in value, your LTV may be worse, potentially increasing your rate.

## Next Steps

Compare current rates using our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}), read our [mortgage types guide](/{code}/blog/mortgage-types-compared/) to understand your options, or check [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/) for quick answers.
"""

def body_investment(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Property Investment Guide — {d['name']} 2026

Property investment can build long-term wealth through rental income and capital appreciation. However, it requires careful analysis of yields, taxes, financing costs, and market dynamics. This guide covers everything you need to know about investing in property in {d['name']}.

## Rental Yields and Market Overview

{d['investment']}

## Financing Investment Property

Investment property financing differs from owner-occupier mortgages. Lenders typically require higher deposits (20-40%), charge higher interest rates (0.5-2% premium), and apply stricter affordability criteria. Rental income may be partially factored into the assessment, but expect conservative treatment. Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to model investment scenarios.

## Tax Implications

Understanding the full tax picture is critical for investment returns. Rental income taxation, capital gains treatment, deductible expenses, and stamp duty or transfer tax surcharges on additional properties all affect your net return. Our [property tax guide](/{code}/blog/property-tax-guide/) provides a comprehensive breakdown of all applicable taxes.

## Due Diligence for Investment

Investment due diligence goes beyond a standard purchase. Research local rental demand, vacancy rates, tenant quality, maintenance requirements, and comparable rental values. Our [property inspection checklist](/{code}/blog/property-inspection-checklist/) covers physical due diligence, while local rental market research is equally important.

## Next Steps

Model your investment returns using our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}), read our [buying costs guide](/{code}/blog/buying-costs-explained/) to understand total acquisition costs, or browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/).
"""

def body_inspection(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Property Inspection Checklist — {d['name']} 2026

A thorough property inspection can save you from costly surprises after purchase. This checklist covers everything to check when buying property in {d['name']}, from structural integrity to legal compliance.

## Professional Inspection

{d['inspection']}

## Your Personal Walkthrough Checklist

Beyond the professional inspection, conduct your own assessment during viewings. Check water pressure in all taps and showers, test all light switches and power outlets, open and close all windows and doors (checking for sticking or misalignment), look for signs of damp or mould (especially in corners, under windows, and in bathrooms), check the condition of floors (soft spots may indicate structural issues), assess natural light at different times of day, and listen for noise from neighbours, roads, or aircraft.

## External Checks

Inspect the roof from ground level for visible damage, missing tiles, or sagging. Check guttering and downpipes. Look at the condition of external walls, windows, and doors. Assess the garden or common areas. Check boundary fences and walls. Verify drainage and check for signs of flooding.

## Location and Neighbourhood

Walk the neighbourhood at different times. Check proximity to transport, schools, shops, and amenities. Research local development plans that could affect value. Check crime statistics and flooding risk maps for the area.

## Legal and Documentation Checks

Verify ownership records, check for any encumbrances or restrictions, confirm planning permission compliance for any extensions or modifications, and review any leasehold or commonhold terms. Our [property buying guide](/{code}/property-buying-guide/) covers the full legal process.

## Next Steps

Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) once you have found the right property, read our [buying costs guide](/{code}/blog/buying-costs-explained/) to budget accurately, or browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/).
"""

def body_mistakes(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Common Property Buying Mistakes in {d['name']} — And How to Avoid Them

Buying property is complex, and first-time buyers and experienced purchasers alike make costly errors. This guide covers the most common mistakes in {d['name']} and how to avoid each one.

## The Top Mistakes

{d['mistakes']}

## Financial Mistakes

The most expensive mistakes are financial. Not comparing mortgage rates across multiple lenders can cost tens of thousands over the life of the loan. Not understanding the full buying costs (taxes, fees, insurance) leads to budget shortfalls at the worst time. Stretching beyond your comfortable payment limit leaves no buffer for rate changes, job changes, or unexpected expenses.

## Process Mistakes

Moving too fast without proper due diligence, or too slow in a competitive market, both have consequences. Skipping professional inspections saves a few hundred but risks thousands in hidden defects. Not getting pre-approved before searching wastes time on properties you cannot afford.

## Legal and Documentation Mistakes

Every property market has legal pitfalls. Not verifying ownership records, missing encumbrances, or failing to check planning compliance can create problems that are expensive or impossible to fix after settlement. Always engage qualified legal representation appropriate to {d['name']}'s requirements.

## How to Protect Yourself

Build a team of professionals: a qualified mortgage advisor or broker, an experienced legal professional, and a trusted building inspector. Their combined fees are a small fraction of the purchase price and protect you from the mistakes above.

## Next Steps

Read our [first home buyer guide](/{code}/blog/first-home-buyer-guide/) for the complete step-by-step process, use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to set a realistic budget, or browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/).
"""

def body_rent_vs_buy(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Rent vs Buy Analysis — {d['name']} 2026

Should you rent or buy? It is one of the most important financial decisions you will make. The answer depends on your financial situation, how long you plan to stay, local market conditions, and personal preferences. This guide provides a framework for the decision in {d['name']}.

## The Financial Comparison

{d['rentVsBuy']}

## Key Factors in the Calculation

The rent-vs-buy decision depends on several interconnected factors. Purchase costs (taxes, fees) create an initial hurdle that takes years to recover through equity building and appreciation. The mortgage rate determines your monthly cost of ownership. Expected property appreciation affects the return on your investment. Length of stay is critical — the longer you stay, the more buying tends to outperform renting. Opportunity cost of the deposit (what it could earn invested elsewhere) favours renting in high-return investment environments.

## Beyond the Numbers

Financial analysis does not capture everything. Ownership provides stability — no rent increases, no landlord selling, freedom to renovate. Renting provides flexibility — easier to relocate for career opportunities, no maintenance responsibility, no exposure to property market downturns.

## Use Our Calculator to Model Your Scenario

Every situation is different. Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to model your specific numbers — monthly repayments at different rates and terms, total interest paid, and how extra payments affect the total cost. Compare the monthly mortgage cost against your current or expected rent to see the real difference.

## Next Steps

If buying looks right for you, read our [first home buyer guide](/{code}/blog/first-home-buyer-guide/) for the complete process. Check government support in our [grants guide](/{code}/blog/government-grants-schemes/). Browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/) for quick answers.
"""

def body_property_tax(code, d, iso):
    return f"""## Property Tax Guide — {d['name']} 2026

Understanding the full tax picture is essential for any property purchase or investment. Taxes affect buying costs, ongoing ownership expenses, rental income, and eventual sale proceeds. This guide covers every tax that applies to property in {d['name']}.

## Taxes When Buying

{d['propertyTax']}

## Ongoing Annual Taxes

Property ownership in {d['name']} involves annual tax obligations beyond the one-off purchase taxes. These typically include local government property charges, which vary by area and property value. Budget for these as a fixed annual cost of ownership alongside insurance and maintenance.

## Taxes on Rental Income

If you rent out your property, the rental income is subject to taxation. The rules for what expenses can be deducted (mortgage interest, maintenance, management fees, depreciation, insurance) vary and can significantly affect your net return. Consult a tax advisor familiar with {d['name']}'s property tax rules for investment properties.

## Taxes When Selling

Capital gains treatment varies between primary residences and investment properties, with many jurisdictions offering preferential treatment or exemptions for primary residences held for a minimum period. Understanding the tax implications before you buy helps you plan your holding period and exit strategy.

## Tax Planning Tips

Work with a tax professional who understands property taxation in {d['name']}. Structure your purchase correctly from the start — the ownership structure (personal, joint, corporate) affects tax treatment. Keep records of all improvements and costs as these may reduce future capital gains liability.

## Next Steps

Use our [mortgage calculator](/calculator/?country={iso}) to factor tax costs into your budget, read our [buying costs guide](/{code}/blog/buying-costs-explained/) for total acquisition costs, or browse [{d['name']} FAQs](/{code}/faqs/).
"""

